# Cultural Landscapes Inventory: Emmitsburg Road Ridge Battlefield (National Park Service, 2024)
# Read: PDF pp. 40-43 (physical history to July 1863 and aftermath), 62-64 (small-scale features: fences),
#       79-83 (buildings and structures), 94-98 (vegetation).
@ NPS-CLI-ERR

# --- 1863 character, battle and aftermath ---
40 | general | Klingel, Trostle and Codori farms and the Neinstedt field: ownership on the eve of the battle | bld-klingle-farm,bld-trostle-farm,bld-codori-farm | 1863-07 | stated | Daniel H. Klingel bought his farm from Jacob Benner on April 2, 1863; the Trostle farm was owned by Peter Trostle and occupied by Abraham Trostle; the Codori farm was leased to John Staub; the Neinstedt field belonged to Henry Conrad Neinstedt | On the eve of the battle, the Klingel Farm was owned by Daniel H. Klingel >>> was owned by Henry Conrad Neinstedt.
40 | woodlots | Codori and Trostle thickets and the Codori Knoll grove | bld-codori-farm,bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07 | stated | In 1863 woody growth stood in the uncultivated areas: bushy growth and isolated clumps of trees in the Codori and Trostle thickets along Plum Run, and the trees of the Codori Knoll grove | Woody vegetation grew in the outlying, uncultivated areas; >>> associated with the Codori Knoll Grove. | #s16
40 | fields | Klingel, Trostle and Codori farms: orchards, gardens, crops | bld-klingle-farm,bld-trostle-farm,bld-codori-farm | 1863-07 | stated | In 1863 the farms had several fruit orchards, flower and vegetable gardens, potato patches, hedgerows, agricultural crops and pastures | There were several fruit orchards, flower and vegetable gardens, potato patches, hedgerows, agricultural crops, and pastures.
40 | fences | Klingel, Trostle and Codori farms: fence types | bld-klingle-farm,bld-trostle-farm,bld-codori-farm,FEN-klingle | 1863-07 | stated | In 1863 fence construction on these farms was typically wooden post and rail, worm, or wooden post and board; a few stone walls were also found within the project area | Typically, fence construction was wooden post and rail, worm, or wooden post and board. A few stone walls were also found within the project area.
40 | roads | Trostle farm lane | bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The Trostle farm lane was part of an earlier settlement road connecting the Taneytown Road with the Black Horse Tavern Road; farm lanes were unimproved | The Trostle Farm Lane was part of a yet earlier settlement road, connecting the Taneytown Road on the east with the Black Horse Tavern Road on the west.
42 | fields | Farms on the Emmitsburg Road Ridge at the time of the battle | bld-klingle-farm,bld-trostle-farm,bld-codori-farm | 1863-07 | stated | At the time of the battle all of the land of these farms was in agricultural production, in the middle of the summer growing season | At the time of the battle, all of the lands at the farms were used for agricultural production. They were in the midst of the summer growing season
42 | fields | Orchards and ripening wheat (a Massachusetts captain, as quoted by the inventory) | - | 1863-07 | stated | A Massachusetts captain recalled cows grazing in pastures and peach and apple orchards adjoining fields of ripening wheat | A Massachusetts captain observed that the battle >>> adjoined fields of ripening wheat.”
42 | hospitals | Trostle farmhouse | bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The area around the Trostle barn was Sickles's headquarters and the Trostle farmhouse was used briefly as a hospital | The area around the Trostle barn, located along Trostle Farm Lane, served as a headquarters for Union General Daniel Sickles and the farmhouse was used briefly as a hospital.
42 | woodlots | Codori and Trostle thickets, character | bld-codori-farm,bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The shrubby Codori and Trostle thickets held small and intermittent clumps of mature trees and large boulders; their low character allowed views across the battlefield | Located throughout the shrubby Codori and Trostle thickets >>> Emmitsburg Road Ridge and the Peach Orchard.
43 | fences | Trostle farm lane gate and fence openings | bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07-02 | stated | Several small openings within the Trostle Farm Lane fences to the west provided Union access to the Peach Orchard battlefront but were less successful as retreat routes: the small gateways proved deadly obstacles, as masses of troops and artillery tried to pass through the small openings while under attack | and several small openings >>> while under attack.
43 | fences | North-south fences on the Emmitsburg Road Ridge farms | FEN-klingle,FEN-emmitsburg-road | 1863-07-02 to 07-03 | stated | Union forces removed many of the north-south fences before the fighting of July 2, but enough fences stood on July 2 and 3 to hinder orderly movement; Confederate advances were slowed as troops climbed fences under fire | Although Union forces removed many of the north-south fences >>> climbed over fencelines under fire.
43 | damage | Trostle and Klingel damage claims | bld-trostle-farm,bld-klingle-farm,FEN-klingle | 1863-07 (claims filed in the 1870s) | stated | The claims of Catherine Trostle and Daniel Klingel listed over 90 acres of field crops, thousands of fence rails and other fencing materials and 50 chickens, and building damage by shells and bullets; the Klingel claim named damage to fruit trees by rifle pits dug around them | In the 1870s, both Catherine Trostle and Daniel Klingel filed claims >>> by rifle pits being dug around them.”
42 | fences | Trostle farm lane gate and fence openings | bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07-02 | hedged | Trostle Farm Lane, Emmitsburg Road and the agricultural fields were important deployment routes for Union troops and artillery, as well as avenues of retreat; the page ends with the words 'The Trostle Farm Lane Field No. 3 Gate', which appear to begin the sentence that continues on the next page about the gate and the small openings in the lane fences | Trostle Farm Lane, Emmitsburg Road, and the agricultural fields >>> Field No. 3 Gate

# --- Fences (small-scale features) ---
62 | fences | Farm fences on the Emmitsburg Road Ridge during the battle | FEN-klingle,FEN-emmitsburg-road | 1863-07 | stated | The wood fences were typically post and rail, post and board, or Virginia worm; some were dismantled before the fighting of July 2 for ease of movement and the rails used for breastworks; others were left partly or wholly standing | Typically, the wood fences were constructed as post and rail, post and board, or Virginia worm. >>> as obstacles for the advancing enemy.
63 | fences | Post and rail fence on both sides of the Emmitsburg Road in front of the Klingel farm | FEN-emmitsburg-road,FEN-klingle,bld-klingle-farm | 1863-07-02 | stated | A wooden post and rail fence stood on both sides of the Emmitsburg Road in front of the Klingel farm; portions were dismantled for troop movement and for breastworks built on the east side of the road north of Klingel's, which also used the Klingel-Codori boundary fence | Wooden post and rail fence along Emmitsburg Road, both sides of road, in front of Klingel Farm: >>> Klingel-Codori property boundary fence.
63 | fences | Trostle lane fencing | bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07-02 | stated | The Trostle lane fencing was not initially removed because it ran east-west; it blocked movement across the fields, trapped Bigelow's 9th Massachusetts battery, served as breastworks at its western end and as cover for the 21st Mississippi | Trostle Lane fencing: Not initially removed because of east-west orientation; >>> attacking Bigelow’s Battery on July 2.
63 | fences | Codori-Neinstedt property boundary fence | bld-codori-farm | 1863-07-02 | stated | A wooden post and rail / Virginia worm fence stood on the Codori-Neinstedt property boundary and confined Willard's brigade in its attack | Wooden post and rail/Virginia worm fence along property boundary between Codori-Neinstedt: Directed and confined Willard’s Union brigade during attack on Barksdale’s Confederate troops.
63 | fences | Fence types replaced 1895-1927 | - | ~1895-1927 change | stated | Over fifty percent of the total fencing extant in 1895 had been replaced with another fence type, or removed and not replaced at all, by 1927; the largest change was the replacement of the old GBMA twisted wire fence with the new GNMPC wooden post and metal pipe fencing, and about half of the Virginia worm fence that had been prevalent was removed or replaced by 1927 | #s20-24
64 | fences | Fences in the late 1930s | FEN-emmitsburg-road | ~late 1930s state | stated | By the late 1930s wooden post and rail fences divided the majority of the project-area farm fields, a few sections were separated by woven wire fences on wooden posts, only a small section of stone wall remained on the three farms, and there were no Virginia worm fences | #s10-12
64 | fences | Emmitsburg Road fences set back in the late 1930s | FEN-emmitsburg-road | ~late 1930s change | stated | In the late 1930s all of the park fences along Emmitsburg Road were set back ten or more feet from the road edge, to improve the appearance of the road, which had been widened several times, and to make maintenance of the side slopes and berms easier | #s16
64 | fences | Fences rebuilt 2000-2012 | - | 2000-2012 reconstruction (modern) | stated | Many fences in this area were rebuilt or rehabilitated in 2000-2012 | Many fences in the project area were rebuilt or rehabilitated in 2000-2012
63 | monuments | Monuments on the Emmitsburg Road Ridge by 1895 and 1927 | - | !1895 (end of the GBMA period) | stated | By the end of the GBMA period in 1895 sixteen monuments and markers had been placed within the project area | #s9
63 | monuments | Monuments on the Emmitsburg Road Ridge by 1895 and 1927 | - | !1927 (end of the GNMPC period) | stated | By the end of the GNMPC period in 1927 ten additional monuments and markers had been added to the project area | #s13

# --- Buildings and structures ---
79 | buildings | Klingel farmhouse in the battle | bld-klingle-farm | 1863-07-02 | stated | The Klingel farmhouse was used by the 73rd New York and the 16th Massachusetts as a fortified position; those troops knocked holes in the chinking between the building's exposed logs and fired upon the advancing Confederates | #s9-10
79 | buildings | Trostle barn in the battle | bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07-02 | stated | The Trostle barn was used as cover by individuals and artillery units; Sickles was carried to its northeast corner after he was wounded | #s12-14
79 | hospitals | Trostle house | bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07 (close of the battle) | stated | The Trostle house was used for hospital purposes at the close of the battle | Although the house was used for hospital purposes at the close of the battle, | #s16
79 | fences | Trostle farm stone wall and gate (Bigelow's battery) | bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07-02 | stated | A stone wall with a narrow gate stood on the Trostle lane; the wall stayed standing, the gate was blocked by an overturned limber and four guns of Bigelow's battery were trapped and captured by the 21st Mississippi | Because the stone wall adjacent to the gate remained standing, >>> captured by the 21st Mississippi Regiment. | #s19
79 | fences | Stone walls at the Codori woodlot and the Neinstedt field | bld-codori-farm | 1863-07 | stated | Other stone walls separated the Codori woodlot and Neinstedt field, and the Neinstedt field and the George Weikart field (the report's spelling) | Other stone walls separated the Codori woodlot and Neinstedt field, and the Neinstedt field and the George Weikart field.
80 | buildings | Klingel farm buildings before and after the battle | bld-klingle-farm | 1863-07 (farmhouse and log barn); other buildings 1880s-1890s | stated | The farmhouse and a log barn were the only original Klingel buildings; a barn, garage/woodshed, smokehouse and implement shed/corn crib were added in the 1880s and 1890s | On the Klingel farm, the farmhouse and the log barn were the only original buildings predating the GBMA period. In the 1880s and 1890s, a barn, garage/woodshed, smokehouse, and implement shed/corn crib were added to the farm.
80 | buildings | Klingel farm as advertised in the mid-1860s | bld-klingle-farm,FEN-klingle | mid-1860s (advertisement after the battle) | stated | Advertisements of the mid-1860s describe the Klingel farm of sixteen acres with a two-storey log farmhouse with attached rear kitchen, a well at the door, a spring, a hog pen, new fencing and a large orchard of apple, peach, pear and cherry trees | According to early real estate advertisements for the sixteen-acre farm, >>> apple, peach, pear, and cherry trees. | #s2
80 | buildings | Klingel log barn | bld-klingle-farm | 1863-07; removed perhaps in the 1880s | hedged | The original log barn may have been removed when the new barn was added in the 1880s | The original log barn may have been removed when the new barn was added in the 1880s.
81 | buildings | Klingel farmhouse (present description) | bld-klingle-farm | ~modern 2024 description of a building dated about 1850 | stated | The Klingel farmhouse is a two-storey log building of 26 by 21 feet on a stone foundation; the park recently removed an 1870s frame addition, a lean-to and the vertical board and batten siding | The building is a two-story log construction that measures 26 by 21 feet on a stone foundation with a shingle covered roof (Figure 28). >>> vertical board and batten siding. | #s14
81 | buildings | Klingel farmhouse walls in July 1863 | bld-klingle-farm | 1863-07-02 | stated | On July 2 men of Carr's brigade fortified the Klingel house and knocked out the chinking of the then exposed log walls to fire on the enemy | On July 2, men of Carr’s Union brigade fortified themselves within the house during Confederate attacks, knocking out the chinking in the then exposed log walls in order to fire against enemy infantry. | #s14
81 | buildings | Klingel barn (present building) | bld-klingle-farm | !1870-1880 | stated | The present Klingel barn (inventory date 1870-1880, contributing) is a one-story barn and loft measuring 42 by 30 feet and 24 feet high, dated after the battle | #s22-23
82 | buildings | Trostle house in 1863 | bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The Trostle house (inventory date 1850s-1860s, contributing) was a two-story hall and parlor style wood frame farmhouse on a granite foundation; an additional wood-framed addition of two rooms downstairs and two rooms upstairs was added on the north end during 1863 and was under construction during the battle | An additional wood-framed addition, consisting of two rooms downstairs and two rooms upstairs, was added on the north end during 1863 and was under construction during the battle. | @81:#s35-36
82 | hospitals | Trostle house (after July 5) | bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07-05 and after | stated | The Trostle house was used for temporary hospital purposes after the Confederate army left on July 5 | It was used for temporary hospital purposes after the evacuation of the Confederate army on July 5. | @81:#s35
82 | buildings | Trostle barn | bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07 (built 1850-1860) | stated | The Trostle barn still exhibits signs of damage caused by a solid shot striking the south gable wall | The barn still exhibits signs of damage caused by a solid shot striking the south gable wall. | #s11
82 | hospitals | Trostle barn (after July 5) | bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07-05 and after | hedged | It is likely that the Trostle barn served temporarily for hospital purposes after the fighting | It is likely that the barn served temporarily for hospital purposes after close of the fighting on July 5. | #s11
82 | buildings | Trostle barn, later alteration | bld-trostle-farm | ~1940s alteration | stated | The lower stable floor of the Trostle barn was altered for dairying in the 1940s; the second floor keeps much original fabric | =Although the second floor of the {{bam|barn}} still includes much original fabric and integrity, the lower stable floor was altered for dairying purposes in the 1940s. | #s11
82 | buildings | Trostle summer kitchen | bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07 (under construction) | stated | The Trostle summer kitchen (inventory date 1862-1863, contributing), a one-story, three-bay brick building on a stone foundation, was under construction during the battle according to photographic evidence | #s26-29
82 | buildings | Trostle carriage house | bld-trostle-farm | !1880-1890; moved to the farm 1937 | stated | The Trostle carriage house (inventory date 1880-1890, contributing) is a one-story, gable roof structure of 12 by 25 feet with board and batten siding on a concrete masonry unit foundation; it was moved from the G. Weikert farm (spelled 'Wiekert' in the report) in 1937 | #s30-33
82 | fences | Field stone wall, Trostle farm | bld-trostle-farm | ~pre-1863 origin; dimensions as surveyed today (modern) | stated | The field stone wall of the Abraham Trostle farm (inventory date pre-1863, contributing) is of dry-laid granite stones of varying width and height, measuring around 1.5 to 3 (the sentence continues on the next page) | #s38-39
82 | buildings | Trostle barn | bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07 (inventory date 1850-1860) | stated | The Trostle barn (inventory date 1850-1860, contributing) is a two-story brick and frame Pennsylvania bank barn on a granite foundation, 80 by 64 feet, with a 6 by 15 feet lean-to used as a wagon shed and corn crib on the south side | =(1850-1860, Contributing) The barn is a two-story brick and frame Pennsylvania bank {{bam|barn}} on a granite foundation that measures 80 by 64 feet with a 6 by 15 feet lean-to used as a wagon shed and corn crib on the south side (see Figure 23). | #s11
83 | fences | Field stone wall, Trostle farm | bld-trostle-farm | ~pre-1863 origin; dimensions as surveyed today (modern) | stated | The dry-laid field wall of the Abraham Trostle farm (pre-1863, granite stones of varying width and height, around 1.5 to 3 feet) has a rider fence atop a portion; its total overall length is 1,200 feet, not including Bigelow's Battery Walls, and it separates fields, running perpendicular to and between United States Avenue and Wheatfield Road just southeast of the farmhouse | A rider fence stands atop a portion of wall. >>> just southeast of the farmhouse. | @82:#s38-39
83 | fences | Bigelow's Battery stone walls (Trostle farm) | bld-trostle-farm | ~pre-1863 origin; dimensions as surveyed today (modern) | stated | Bigelow's Battery Stone Walls (inventory date pre-1863, contributing) form an L-shaped wall in front of and across the road from the A. Trostle farmhouse, about 4 feet wide, 2 feet high and 960 feet long, built by Peter Trostle but associated with the A. Trostle Farm during the battle | #s12-16
83 | fences | Boundary stone wall, Neinstedt field | - | ~pre-1863 origin; dimensions as surveyed today (modern) | stated | The Boundary Stone Wall of the Conrad Neinstedt Field (inventory date pre-1863, contributing) is a dry-laid granite wall of varying size stones, 3-4 feet in width by 2.5-3 feet in height, forming an L with an east-west section running parallel to United States Avenue just west of George Weikert's farmhouse; the east-west section has a rider fence atop the stone wall | #s17-20
80 | fences | Trostle farm stone wall rebuilt by 1927 | bld-trostle-farm | ~rebuilt by 1927 | hedged | A small section of stone wall on the Trostle farm was rebuilt by 1927, probably replicating an earlier lost feature: present walls are partly rebuilt | By 1927, a small section of stone wall was rebuilt on the Trostle farm, probably replicating an earlier feature that had since been lost.

# --- Vegetation ---
94 | woodlots | Codori-Trostle thicket at the time of the battle | bld-codori-farm,bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The Codori-Trostle thicket was primarily a sparsely wooded area with small intermittent clumps of mature trees; meadow and pasture at its northern and southern ends; shrubs along Plum Run, thick enough with battle smoke to affect observation on July 2 | At the time of the battle the Codori-Trostle Thicket was primarily a sparsely wooded area >>> infantry combat on July 2.
94 | woodlots | Views over the Codori-Trostle thicket | - | 1863-07-03 | stated | Views between the Emmitsburg Road and Cemetery Ridge over the thicket were relatively unobstructed | Views between Emmitsburg Road and Cemetery Ridge over the Codori-Trostle Thicket, however, were relatively unobstructed.
95 | woodlots | Codori Knoll grove (Cemetery Ridge) | bld-codori-farm | 1863-07 | stated | A grove of mature trees dominated the Codori Knoll in the middle of Cemetery Ridge and gave cover to Union troops | Just northeast of the Codori-Trostle Thicket, a grove of mature trees dominated the Codori Knoll in the middle of Cemetery Ridge.
95 | orchards | Codori orchard | bld-codori-farm | 1863-07 | stated | A large orchard stood on the Codori farm east of the farm buildings | Additionally, a large orchard was extant on the Codori farm east of the farm buildings.
95 | woodlots | Large tree at the Codori farm lane | bld-codori-farm | 1863-07-03 | stated | A large tree near the junction of the Codori farm lane and the Emmitsburg Road served as a Confederate sharpshooter's post on July 3 | A large tree near the junction of the Codori farm lane and Emmitsburg Road served as a Confederate sharpshooter’s post on July 3.
95 | orchards | Klingel orchards | bld-klingle-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The Klingel farm had two orchards, one south and east of the farmhouse and one north of it; both were mostly apple, with cherry, pear and peach trees also in the north orchard | Both orchards contained mostly apple trees, but cherry, pear, and peach trees were also extant in the north orchard. | #s13
95 | orchards | Klingel orchards, positions | bld-klingle-farm | 1863-07-02 | stated | One Klingel orchard lay south and east of the farmhouse, the other north of the farmhouse | One orchard, located south and east of the Klingel farmhouse, >>> during the artillery duel on the afternoon of July 2.
95 | orchards | Trostle orchards | bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The Trostle farm had two orchards: an apple and cherry orchard north of the barn and another south of the barn | #s16-17
95 | orchards | Trostle south orchard | bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07 | hedged | The Trostle orchard south of the barn was most likely immature or already destroyed before July 2 | This orchard was most likely immature or had already been destroyed before a shot was fired on July 2. | #s17
95 | woodlots | Large oak at the Trostle lane (Sickles's position) | bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07-02 | stated | The only other vegetation mentioned on the Trostle farm was a large oak tree located just south of Trostle Lane, which designated the location of General Sickles and his staff on July 2 | #s20-21
95 | fields | Crops on the farms after the battle | - | ~1864-1895 (after the battle) | stated | After the battle farmers kept small fields with consistent plantings of wheat, corn, oats and hay | Farmers continued to maintain a series of small, easily workable fields on the farms with consistent crop plantings of wheat, corn, oats, and hay.
95 | woodlots | Vegetation along Plum Run during the battle and after | - | 1863-07; expanded by 1895 | stated | During the battle the vegetation along the creek (Plum Run) had been sparse enough for the opposing armies to see and maneuver through; by the end of the GBMA period the thicket had begun to expand and fill in, especially along fence lines projecting out into the adjoining pastures and fields | #s28-29 | #s25
96 | woodlots | Tree planting by the park commission, 1915-1916 | - | ~1915-1916 planting | stated | The tree-planting program of the park commission was at its height during 1915 and 1916, when approximately 450 trees were installed; the Codori and Trostle farms were mentioned as locations of the new trees; by 1927 the thicket had nearly doubled from its mid-1880s size and the tree cover was thick, forming a nearly impenetrable woodland along nearly the entire length of Plum Run | #s12-16
96 | orchards | Orchards gone by the mid-1930s | bld-klingle-farm,bld-trostle-farm | ~1930s state | stated | By the mid-1930s neither farm supported an orchard (the passage describes the vegetation on the three farms) | #s28-29
97 | woodlots | Tree clearing of 1963-1964 after the Warren map | - | ~1963-1964 clearing | stated | In 1963-1964 the park removed trees and brush using the 1868-1869 Warren map survey as a guide | During the fall and winter of 1963-1964, the NPS selectively removed trees and brush from areas of the battlefield, using the 1868-1869 Warren Map Survey as a guide.
97 | orchards | Trostle and Klingel orchards replanted | bld-klingle-farm,bld-trostle-farm | 2005 and 2009 replanting (modern) | stated | The Trostle apple orchard was replanted in 2005 and the Klingel apple orchard in 2009 | Significant changes came to both farms when the park completed a replanting of the Trostle apple orchard in 2005 and the Klingel apple orchard in 2009.
97 | woodlots | Codori-Trostle thicket rehabilitated | - | 2002-2009 rehabilitation (modern) | stated | Between 2002 and 2009 the wooded thicket was rehabilitated and the surrounding woods removed | Between 2002 and 2009, the wooded thicket was rehabilitated, and the surrounding woods were removed.
97 | orchards | Orchards of the battlefield farms | - | 1863-07 | stated | Almost every farm of any size had an orchard, most of them planted on acreage proportionate to the size of the farm | #s24-25
97 | orchards | Klingel and Trostle orchards (replanted 2009 and 2005) | - | 1863-07 (replanting dates 2009 and 2005) | stated | Two orchards existed in the Emmitsburg Road Ridge area during the battle: the Klingel Orchard (replanted in 2009, in the southern two-thirds of Klingel Field #2) and the Trostle Orchard (replanted in 2005, in the eastern part of Trostle Field #9) | #s28-31
